---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
const guide = {
  "title": "Visa Refusal: Read the Decision, Review Options & Reapply",
  "description": "Use a refusal-response workflow tied to the actual decision, official review rights and relevant evidence rather than a generic appeal template.",
  "intro": "Use a refusal-response workflow tied to the actual decision, official review rights and relevant evidence rather than a generic appeal template.",
  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "US Department of State visa denials",
      "url": "https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/us-visas/visa-information-resources/visa-denials.html"
    },
    {
      "title": "UK appeal overview",
      "url": "https://www.gov.uk/immigration-asylum-tribunal"
    },
    {
      "title": "European Commission application guidance",
      "url": "https://home-affairs.ec.europa.eu/policies/schengen/visa-policy/applying-schengen-visa_en"
    }
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Preserve the record",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Save the decision, receipt date, submitted application and evidence. Identify the provision cited and each factual concern. A refusal, document request and administrative processing are not interchangeable. Check the next action before paying for another application.",
        "Create a response table: stated concern, evidence already supplied, correction or new evidence, next action. This focuses the response on the real problem rather than adding unrelated travel stamps or bank statements."
      ],
      "source": 0
    },
    {
      "heading": "US: read the refusal provision",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The Department of State explains that a 214(b) refusal has no appeal; a new application requires the relevant steps and fee, and significant changes should be considered. A 221(g) refusal can involve missing information or administrative processing. Follow the actual consular instructions."
      ],
      "source": 0
    },
    {
      "heading": "UK: check the stated rights",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Appeal and administrative-review eligibility depend on the decision category. The official overview does not give every visitor applicant a general appeal. Use the decision letter and named procedure. Get qualified advice promptly where eligibility or deadlines are unclear."
      ],
      "source": 1
    },
    {
      "heading": "Schengen and other countries",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Use the refusal form’s country-specific appeal instructions. There is no universal A-to-I code scheme or shared deadline. For Canada or another destination, use that authority’s decision and procedures rather than importing UK or US rules."
      ],
      "source": 2
    },
    {
      "heading": "Prepare an honest next step",
      "paragraphs": [
        "If reapplying, address the actual concern with relevant, accurate evidence and explain any corrected error or genuine change. Answer refusal-history questions honestly. Do not falsely change travel purpose or conceal earlier decisions.",
        "Where a dispute concerns legal error, inadmissibility or misrepresentation, consult a qualified professional. A new cover letter cannot guarantee approval. No universal judicial-review deadline, waiting period or success rate is quoted here."
      ],
      "source": 0
    }
  ]
};
---
<GuideLayout {...guide} />
